<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Alma's privacy manifest.

  This file must match the App Privacy answers typed into App Store Connect,
  because Xcode builds a privacy report from this manifest plus every SDK's, and
  that report is what the answers are checked against. The two are derived from
  the same document: `mobile/store/APP-PRIVACY.md`, which cites the file and line
  behind every row. Change one, change both.

  It lives inside `Alma/` on purpose. That folder is a
  PBXFileSystemSynchronizedRootGroup (`Alma.xcodeproj/project.pbxproj:14-20`), so
  a file dropped in joins the target automatically and a resource lands at the
  root of the app bundle — which is the only place an app target's privacy
  manifest is read from. `Info.plist` is kept outside the folder for the opposite
  reason: it would be copied in as a stray second plist. Verify once in Xcode
  (target -> Build Phases -> Copy Bundle Resources) that this file is listed; the
  project file is hand-written and its synchronized group has no exception list.

  Every entry below is "linked: true" and "tracking: false", and neither is a
  default. `api/deps.py:61-91` mints an account row on the first call from any
  client, so there is no anonymous request in this product and nothing here was
  de-identified before collection — Apple's test for "not linked". And there is
  no advertising identifier, no ad network, no analytics SDK and no data broker
  anywhere in the codebase, which is what keeps every tracking flag false and
  what lets the app ship with no ATT prompt.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>

	<!--
	  Must be empty while NSPrivacyTracking is false. The day a domain appears
	  here, the app needs an ATT prompt before it may be contacted.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<!--
	  One of the five required-reason API families is used, and it became one on
	  7 August 2026: `UserDefaults`, which now holds the installation id
	  (`Networking/InstallationId.swift`). This block was an empty array with a
	  comment saying "no UserDefaults — the bearer token is in the Keychain",
	  which was true and is no longer the whole truth. An app that touches
	  `UserDefaults` without a declared reason is emailed ITMS-91053 at upload,
	  so this is a submission blocker rather than a nicety.

	  `CA92.1` is the right code and the narrowest one: read and write
	  information accessible only to this app itself. The id is written by this
	  app, read by this app, and lives in no app group and no shared container —
	  which is also why it is not `1C8F.1` (an app group) or `C56D.1` (a
	  third-party SDK's own defaults).

	  The other four families are still unused. Grepped across `mobile/ios/`: no
	  file-timestamp API, no `systemUptime` or `mach_absolute_time`, no
	  `volumeAvailableCapacity`, no `activeInputModes`. The one `FileManager`
	  call in the app is `temporaryDirectory`, used to write the person's own
	  data export so the share sheet can hand it to them
	  (`Screens/Settings/AccountModel.swift:201`), and it is not on any list.

	  The app links no third-party framework at all (`project.pbxproj` declares no
	  framework build phase), so there is no SDK manifest to merge into this one.
	  That fact stops being true the moment a dependency is added.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
		  The account. Null until somebody signs in; passwordless throughout, so
		  there is no credential beside it. `db/models.py:99-131`, and held in
		  clear on the sign-in row at `models.py:524-534`.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!--
		  Two names: the display name on the account (`models.py:99-131`) and the
		  name on a birth profile, which numerology actually reads
		  (`calc/service.py:181`). A profile may be about somebody else — a
		  compatibility reading needs a second birth (`routers/readings.py:375-401`).
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!--
		  The birthplace coordinate, stored as full-precision floats
		  (`models.py:134-164`). Not a device reading: the app asks for no location
		  permission, imports no CoreLocation, and the coordinate is picked from a
		  search box against a bundled offline gazetteer (`alma/geo.py:1-20`) whose
		  lookups never leave the server. Declared because Apple's definition is
		  about resolution, and this is a coordinate about a person.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePreciseLocation</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!--
		  OWNER'S CALL — the one judgement on this form.

		  Nothing here solicits or infers any category Apple enumerates. But
		  `chat_message.body` is 2000 unfiltered characters a turn
		  (`models.py:457-488`, `api/schemas.py:172`) and `memory` stores what the
		  model extracted from it (`models.py:491-508`), so a person may put a
		  belief, a diagnosis or a relationship into either; and a divination
		  product sits close to "religious or philosophical beliefs" however it is
		  framed. Declared on the conservative reading. If the owner decides
		  otherwise, delete this dict AND change the answer in App Store Connect —
		  birth data stays declared either way, under Other Data Types below.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeSensitiveInfo</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!--
		  Chat messages and thread titles (`models.py:457-488`,
		  `routers/readings.py:772`), the remembered facts (`models.py:491-508`),
		  and the generated readings themselves (`models.py:405-438`).
		  Personalization is on the list honestly: memory exists so Alma does not
		  ask in March what was answered in January.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeProductPersonalization</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!--
		  Our own account id, `secrets.token_urlsafe(16)` (`models.py:61-63`),
		  minted by an act rather than by a launch (`api/deps.py`, `current_user`).
		  Analytics is listed because the account id is one of the two
		  attributions a funnel event carries (`alma/funnel.py`) — the other is
		  the installation id below, and there is nothing else to join on.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!--
		  The installation id — new on 7 August 2026, and this file previously
		  said in as many words that no device identifier of any kind was
		  collected.

		  It is a UUID this app generates at first launch and keeps in
		  `UserDefaults` (`Networking/InstallationId.swift`), sent as
		  `X-Alma-Anon` on every request. It exists because the account is no
		  longer minted by opening the app: `GET /v1/auth/session` was called
		  unconditionally at launch and it mints, so every install was an account
		  before anybody had typed anything. Answering "of the people who opened
		  the app, how many finished the journey" without that needs *something*
		  to tell one install from another, and this is the smallest honest
		  something.

		  **Device ID rather than User ID**, because Apple's definition is about
		  what the identifier is attached to rather than about what we call it: a
		  copy of the app, not a person. **Linked: true**, because the moment an
		  account is created out of this install the server records the join
		  (`funnel.claim`) — it is unlinked right up until it is not, and
		  declaring the easier half would be declaring the half that is true for
		  people who never convert.

		  **Tracking: false**, and every clause of Apple's definition is checked
		  rather than assumed: it is not an advertising identifier and there is
		  no ad network in the product; it is never joined with data from another
		  company, because nothing leaves our own backend; it is not a data
		  broker's and it is not sold. `NSPrivacyTracking` above stays false and
		  the app still needs no ATT prompt.

		  It is not the bearer token and must never be confused with one — the
		  token is a credential and lives in the Keychain
		  (`Networking/TokenStore.swift`); this authorises nothing, which is
		  exactly why it is in `UserDefaults`. It is deleted with the account, it
		  goes when the app is deleted, and the client re-mints it after
		  `funnel.PURGE_AFTER_DAYS`, so it cannot outlive the rows it keys.
		-->
		<!--
		  THE SECOND IDENTIFIER UNDER THIS TYPE IS THE APNS DEVICE TOKEN, and it
		  is why `NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality` is on the
		  list below alongside Analytics. Apple's form takes one entry per data
		  *type*, so both identifiers share this dict and the purposes are the
		  union of theirs — but they are otherwise unrelated and it is worth
		  being explicit about which is which:

		  * the anon UUID above is *ours*, minted by the app, for Analytics, and
		    the reason the funnel can count installs;
		  * the **push token** is *Apple's*, issued to this install by APNs,
		    held only while the daily is switched on, and used for exactly one
		    thing — delivering it. That is App Functionality.

		  The push token is registered by `Daily/DailyModel.registerIfPossible`
		  through `POST /v1/notifications/devices`, deleted by
		  `POST /v1/notifications/devices/delete` the moment somebody turns the
		  daily off or the OS revokes the permission, deleted with the account
		  (`accounts.erase` → `notify.tokens.forget`), and swept server-side
		  after ninety days without the app being opened. Tracking stays false:
		  it is not an advertising identifier, it is never joined with another
		  company's data, and it never leaves our backend except to Apple, who
		  issued it.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!--
		  `purchase` (`models.py:259-315`) and `entitlement` (`models.py:167-256`):
		  what was bought, when, the amount, the currency, the country, and Apple's
		  own transaction id. No payment instrument ever reaches us — Apple is the
		  merchant of record and the signed transaction is verified locally against
		  the pinned Apple Root CA G3 (`billing/appstore.py:106+`).
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurchaseHistory</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!--
		  Nine funnel stage names from a closed set (`funnel.py:124-142`) with an
		  eight-key property allowlist (`funnel.py:154-156`) — no IP, no user agent,
		  no referrer, no URL, no device id, no free text. Plus the daily counters
		  (`models.py:511-521`), which are App Functionality rather than Analytics:
		  they are what makes a free tier countable (`routers/readings.py:75-79`).
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!--
		  Birth date and birth time, and there is nowhere else for them to go.

		  Apple's fourteen categories have no type for a date of birth, and this is
		  the most sensitive field in the product: `birth_date`, and `birth_time` as
		  an exact HH:MM wall clock (`models.py:134-164`). Also the IANA time zone
		  derived from the birthplace, the birthplace label, and the optional
		  one-word relationship label on a profile about another person. When App
		  Store Connect asks what "Other" is, that sentence is the answer to type.

		  This entry stays whether or not Sensitive Info above stays. A taxonomy
		  with no box for a birth time is not a reason to leave a birth time
		  undeclared.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDataTypes</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
